AB  - Advancements in Artificial Intelligence (AI) are changing various sectors, and the transportation sector is a key area for innovation. Autonomous Vehicles (AVs) utilize AI technologies such as deep learning, computer vision, and image processing. As these vehicles become increasingly important for future mobility, assessing their implementation readiness becomes essential. Public acceptance of AVs plays a critical role in shaping policies and strategies for the successful integration of AVs into the transportation ecosystem in the future. This research aims to assess public acceptance of AVs, which is critical for their successful integration in the early stages. A conceptual model has been designed based on theoretical models. The research employed both quantitative and qualitative research methods. The primary method of data collection was an online survey conducted in Saudi Arabia. The sample comprised 112 males and 96 females, demonstrating a fair representation of both genders in the study. The results show that perceived usefulness, performance expectancy, and cost were among the most significant factors of adoption intention. Additionally, the participants’ primary concerns about AVs include malfunctions, infrastructure limitations, as well as security and privacy concerns. The results underscore the need for public participation, especially in the early stages, to shape AV acceptance in the region. This offers valuable guidance for policymakers and transport stakeholders working to facilitate future adoption.
ER  - 

