AB  - When crossing the street, maximum pedestrians initiate eye contact with the driver when they perceive an oncoming car. This is one of the traditional methods of ensuring that the driver has noticed you. It is critical to be visible to the driver to cross the street safely and maintain trust and acceptance between pedestrians and fully autonomous vehicles. Assume the same circumstance, except instead of making eye contact with the driver, you realise that the vehicle is without one of the drivers. Would you ever prefer to cross the road without establishing a foundation of trust and acceptability of new technology to feel a sense of security? This paper focuses on and addresses the role of trust and technology acceptability in influencing road users' crossing decisions to offer a sense of security.
ER  - 

