AB  - The increasing deployment of autonomous systems in transportation is reshaping how mobility systems operate and how regulatory frameworks must evolve to facilitate the shift from human-driven to AI-operated systems. This study presents a comparative analysis of regulatory discourse in two transport sectors at the frontier of automation: Autonomous Vehicles (AVs) and Maritime Autonomous Surface Ships (MASS). We used Natural Language Processing (NLP) and lifecycle phase mapping to extract and visualize the distribution of regulatory attention across key governance stages from standards development to testing, certification, liability and international harmonization. Gradient spectrum plots and semantic concept maps revealed significant asymmetries. These findings suggest a temporal lag in maritime governance and inform the importance of differentiated and phase-aware governance strategies in cross-sectoral technological transitions.
