AB  - Connected and autonomous vehicles (CAVs) rely on continuous cloud–edge–vehicle communication, making identity and access management a critical security challenge. Traditional solutions such as PKI or blockchain-based authentication provide integrity but lack a unified, hierarchical trust model. This paper introduces the Vehicle Directory Service (VDS), a lightweight, Active Directory-inspired framework for secure credential issuance and authentication in connected vehicle ecosystems. VDS extends the Kerberos ticket-based mechanism to a distributed Cloud–Edge–Vehicle hierarchy, enabling short-lived, context-bound credentials with low computation and latency overhead. Experimental evaluation on a SUMO–NS3 testbed demonstrates that VDS achieves 96.7% authentication success rate, reduces authentication latency by 68%, and maintains less than 8% CPU utilization, outperforming existing centralized and blockchain-based models. The proposed framework establishes a scalable, low-latency, and secure identity foundation for next-generation intelligent transportation networks.
ER  - 

