AB  - Autonomous vehicles (AVs) are bringing a massive revolution in leveraging advancements in the fields of sensor technologies, artificial intelligence and machine learning algorithms. This paper focuses on providing extensive details on technical foundations, ethical concerns, legal challenges, and societal implications. Key technologies including perception systems, decisionmaking algorithms and experimental frameworks are analysed to understand their role in enabling safe and efficient autonomy. Global regulatory discrepancies, privacy problems, and ethical quandaries in accident scenarios are also examined. The difficulties experienced by systems such as Waymo’s autonomous taxis and Tesla’s Full-Self Driving Beta are demonstrated by real-world case studies. The results highlight the significance of filling key research gaps in the areas of societal repercussions, legal uniformity, and scalability. The future of autonomous vehicles is expected to be inclusive, safe, and morally upright through the promotion of interdisciplinary cooperation. It also considers the economic implications of widespread AV adoption and particularly the impact on employment and infrastructure transformation when making efforts to address these challenges. Exploration of the integration of AVs in existing urban structure which has emphasized the need for adaptive traffic management and energy-efficient solutions have also been done. Trust and acceptance must also be built by raising public awareness and educating about the capabilities and limitations of AVs.
ER  - 

